.TH GPU 2
.SH NAME
gpu: new, available \- an execution-target command language for krylov(2) operators
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "gpu.m";
gpu := load Gpu Gpu->PATH;
Backend: import gpu;

b := gpu->new();
err := b.cmd("device gpu\enprecision f32\enresident on");
apply := b.apply(m);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Gpu
is an execution-target axis, independent of
.IR mesh (2)'s
geometry vocabulary and
.IR krylov (2)'s
algorithm vocabulary: which of those two a solve uses is a property of
the equation being solved; where the resulting matvec actually runs is
a property of the machine, and
.I Gpu
is the one place that distinction lives.
.PP
A
.B Backend
is configured by a small command language, the same shape
.IR tk (2)'s
own widget
.B cmd
and
.IR krylov (2)'s
own
.B Solver.cmd
already use \(em one verb-plus-args string per call (or several,
newline-separated, in one call), string result:
.TP
.BI "device cpu|gpu"
Where the matvec runs. Default
.BR cpu .
.TP
.BI "precision f32|f64"
A real tradeoff, and genuinely enforced. GPU compute is f32-only
(Metal has no
.BR double ),
so this verb decides whether the hardware path may be used at all:
.B apply
asks
.IR gpu (3)
what it would actually compute in, and if that is less precise than
what was asked for, it declines the device and runs on the
.B cpu
instead, saying so through
.BR lasterror ,
rather than quietly returning single precision. Asking for
.B f32
and getting a device that computes in
.B f64
is never a problem, only the reverse. Default
.BR f64 ,
which therefore means
.B "device gpu"
alone will
.B not
use the GPU \(em ask for
.B "precision f32"
explicitly when the loss is acceptable. The cost is real and visible:
an
.IR fem (2)
Poisson solve at
.B f32
converges in 9 CG iterations to a residual near 1e\-11, against 7
iterations and 1e\-17 at
.BR f64 .
.TP
.BI "resident on|off"
Keep an uploaded matrix's GPU buffers alive across calls. Default
.BR on
\(em the whole reason to offload a Krylov solve at all is that
.B matvec
runs every iteration against the
.B same
matrix, so paying the upload cost once and only exchanging the vector
each call is what makes offload worth doing, not a one-shot
convenience.
.PP
.B Apply
builds a
.IR krylov (2)
.B Apply
closure for a
.IR sparse (2)
matrix
.IR m ,
backed by whichever device
.I b
is currently configured for.
.B Apply
never silently substitutes
.B cpu
for a
.B gpu
that was asked for but can't be honoured \(em whether because
.IR gpu (3)
isn't reachable at all, or because it computes in less precision than
.B precision
called for (see above).
.B b.lasterror
is set afterward to say which of those happened (e.g.
.RI \(lq backend: " " "device gpu requested but unavailable, using cpu" \(rq),
or left
.B nil
if the call ran exactly as configured \(em the same observable-not-
swallowed convention
.IR pde (2)/ fem (2)
already use for a solver that failed to converge, applied here to a
backend that couldn't be honoured instead. When the GPU
.B is
used, the matrix is uploaded to it once and only the vector crosses
per
.IR krylov (2)
iteration, which is what
.B resident
above refers to.
.PP
.B Available
is a real check, not a promise: it attempts to reach
.IR gpu (3),
the native device
.B apply
uses when
.I b
is configured for
.BR "device gpu" .
If
.IR gpu (3)
can't be reached (not this platform, or the device otherwise
unavailable),
.B device
.B gpu
still works \(em
.B apply
falls back to
.B cpu
through
.BR lasterror ,
observably, exactly as if it had been asked for in the first place.
.SH "USING IT FROM fem(2)"
.IR fem (2)'s
own
.B newproblem
spec grammar reuses
.I Gpu's
vocabulary verbatim as an optional
.B backend
line, the same way it already reuses
.IR krylov (2)'s
own
.B solver
vocabulary:
.EX
	mesh 20x20x20 domain 1x1x1
	material diffusivity 1.0
	bc dirichlet 0.0
	solver cg tolerance 1e-8
	backend gpu precision f32
.EE
.IR Fem (2)'s
.B solve
reads
.IR p . backend . lasterror
after solving, so a caller of
.I fem
alone never has to import
.I Gpu
just to notice a requested backend fell back.
.SH SOURCE
.B /module/gpu.m
.br
.B /appl/lib/gpu.b
.PP
The device
.B apply
talks to when configured for
.B "device gpu"
is
.IR gpu (3)
\(em a real Styx device (matching
.IR ssl (3)'s
own shape: open a handle, upload a matrix once, exchange only the
vector per call). On this platform its kernel is a real Metal compute
pipeline, one GPU thread per matrix row; on a platform without one it
runs the same matvec on the CPU instead, with nothing in this
interface differing either way except what
.B precision
reports. See
.IR gpu (3)
for the device protocol and how that split is arranged.
.SH SEE ALSO
